;;; A no-break space inside a name splits it in two — M1f
;;; **W73**, register row `W73/nbsp-in-a-name`, filed by S1f.5.22 (T1f.5.22.2,
;;; 2026-11-10).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The last fact writes `k`, U+00A0, a no-break space, and `j` where one
;;; value goes, so it reads as one name to a reader who cannot see the space.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A character that is not ASCII is written only inside a string literal,
;;; after `:why`, `:source` or `:goal-text`, or in a comment (the user's
;;; ruling of 2026-11-10, and Q-M1f.65 (b) for a comment): anywhere else
;;; the program is refused at the lexer, at the character, by a line that
;;; names it and its code point and says where Unicode is allowed, and every
;;; command exits 1 (`ein test`: 2). A Unicode space is no exception: a
;;; separator is ASCII whitespace.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `724c11e8`, release: the program loads, the no-break space a separator,
;;; so the fact is `(p k j)`, two values, R eight states, exit 0 on every
;;; command.

(relation a Thing :why "{?1} is an a")
(relation p Thing :why "{?1} is a p")
(is-a k Thing)
(is-a j Thing)
(a k)
(p k j)
